<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Grunwald</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1410">1410</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Tannenberg (1410), First Battle of Tannenberg, Battle of Žalgiris · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-teutonic-war">Polish–Lithuanian–Teutonic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Grunwald, fought on 15 July 1410 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, was one of the largest battles of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. An allied army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> and Vytautas crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, killing Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen">Ulrich von Jungingen</a> and much of the Order's leadership. The Order survived, but its military power never recovered.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald.jpg" alt="Battle of Grunwald" width="320" height="143" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Matejko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Matejko,_Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1230 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, a crusading military order, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a> and launched the Prussian Crusade, conquering and converting the pagan Prussians by the 1280s. It then turned on the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> and for about 100 years raided its lands, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a>, which separated the order's lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from its branch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, but gained very little territory. In May 1409 an uprising began in Teutonic-held Samogitia, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> supported. Grand Master Ulrich von Jungingen declared war on both on 6 August 1409.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Polish and Lithuanian troops gathered at Czerwińsk and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> over a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontoon-bridge">pontoon bridge</a> between 24 and 30 June 1410. They then marched towards Marienburg (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a>), the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and crossed the Prussian border on 9 July. No contemporary source gives reliable troop counts; the highest estimates, 39,000 Polish–Lithuanian and 27,000 Teutonic men, are often cited as commonly accepted. The Teutonic army was outnumbered but had advantages in discipline, training and equipment.</p><p>On 15 July the armies met between the villages of Grünfelde, Tannenberg and Ludwigsdorf. Vytautas attacked the Teutonic left flank, and after more than an hour the Lithuanian light cavalry retreated. Heavy fighting between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and Teutons followed, and Jungingen led 16 banners to the Polish right flank. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> returned and struck from the rear, Jungingen was killed, and the leaderless Teutonic forces retreated to their camp, where the defence was broken. The battle lasted about ten hours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victors delayed three days on the battlefield and reached Marienburg only on 26 July, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen">Heinrich von Plauen</a> time to organise its defence. Many Teutonic fortresses surrendered, but the siege of Marienburg failed, and the Peace of Thorn of 1411 cost the order minimal territory. The order never recovered its former power, and the burden of war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> caused internal conflicts and an economic downturn in its lands.</p><p>The battle shifted the balance of power in Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> towards the Polish–Lithuanian union. It is regarded as one of the most important victories in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and is also commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>. In 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> named a victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the Battle of Tannenberg and framed it as revenge for 1410.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Grunwald” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vytautas suffered a heavy defeat against the Tatar commander Edigu at the Battle of the Vorskla River in 1399, but in 1410 his troops fought beside the Poles of Władysław II Jagiełło at the <b>Battle of Grunwald</b>, breaking the power of the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Order the burden of taxation and arbitrary rule bred resistance: the Chełmno Land nobility founded the Lizard League in 1397, and after the <b>Battle of Grunwald</b> in 1410 and the heavy compensation of the 1411 Peace of Thorn, nobles and burghers met in a joint assembly in 1412.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Order's military reputation collapsed at the <b>Battle of Grunwald</b> (Tannenberg) on 15 July 1410, when a Polish-Lithuanian army under Władysław II Jagiełło and Vytautas killed Grand Master Ulrich von Jungingen and much of the Order's leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the historian Jan Długosz, Polish knights sang it at the <b>Battle of Grunwald</b> in 1410 and before other notable battles in the years that followed; it also accompanied the coronation of Władysław III of Poland and of the first Jagiellonian kings.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bogurodzica">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)</a></li><li><blockquote>Together, Poland and Lithuania crushed the Teutonic Knights at <b>Grunwald</b> in 1410, and the Second Peace of Thorn in 1466 brought Royal Prussia and the port of Gdansk under the Crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1385-1569">Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569)</a></li><li><blockquote>Family members served as knights and administrators, and Heinrich von Plauen defended Marienburg after the <b>Battle of Grunwald</b> and became Grand Master of the State of the Teutonic Order.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-plauen">Lordship of Plauen</a></li></ul></section>
